@flexiarg test-registry/meter-the-saving
@title Meter What Was Saved, Including When Nothing Was
@keywords meters, cost, wall-time, overhead, honest-report, re-pricing
@audience registry operators, lane-policy authors
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; test_registry.clj cost fields distinguish execution from record-check and fingerprint/registration overhead)
@references [test-registry/spot-check-at-the-declared-rate hygiene/settle-with-meters]
@see-also buffer-cleaner/yield-settled
@why the registry exists to save repeated execution; whether it does is an empirical claim. The only measured round trip shows no wall-time saving: JVM startup, fingerprinting and evidence API costs dominated a three-test namespace (author wrapper 3378 ms; reviewer total 4599 ms for one test).
@how every review record carries meters: tests executed by the reviewer, tests the author's warrant covered, execution time, record-check time, fingerprint and registration overhead, and the lane per namespace. Report saving as a measured difference against the author's recorded full-namespace execution, never against an assumed rerun; report "no saving" plainly. Declared rates (spot-check count, lanes) are re-priced from these meters.
@violation-signature a saving claimed without meters; overhead omitted; a small-namespace demo generalised to large suites; rates never revisited.
! conclusion: Each review reports measured execution and overhead against the author's recorded run, and states when there was no saving.

  + context: Registry-mediated reviews accumulate.

  + IF:
    Anyone wants to know whether the registry pays for itself.

  + HOWEVER:
    Overhead is fixed per review while execution scales with suite size, so small namespaces lose and large ones win; averages hide both.

  + THEN:
    Meter per namespace and per lane, and re-price declared rates from the meters.

  + BECAUSE:
    A saving asserted without meters is the same unreliable narrator the registry was built to replace.

  + evidence: TN-test-registry-2026-09-14 cost table (no total saving on 3 tests); 2026-09-16 reviewer reruns of 5 to 86 tests per WM delivery, with a P10 part 2 run of 9011 assertions, where fixed overhead would be small relative to execution.
